\begin{abstract}
Feature attributions are usually computed once, by a randomised, budget-limited estimator, and read as if they were the attribution.
We ask how far one such SHAP run can be treated as a reliable basis for the conclusion a reader draws from it, the top feature, the top-$k$ set or a sign, rather than for its average error.
With the value function fixed and the exact Shapley value of that value function as reference, we measure per instance how often a run returns the exact top-3 set, with and without tolerance for near ties, on five tabular datasets and four model classes.
At the \texttt{shap} default budget one run returns the top-3 set for $0.90$--$1.00$ of the instances of each cell on the two larger datasets, and for all instances once swaps across near ties are forgiven; failures are the small-margin instances, and mean correlation with the exact values would not have warned of them.
Two runs of the explainer agree far more often than two backgrounds or two retrained models do, so for these conclusions one run is not the weakest link; a one-run certificate is not better than running twice.
Below about three times as many coalitions as features, or with feature-selecting settings, one run is not reliable.
\end{abstract}

